{"title":"Change-Centric Management of Versions in an XML Warehouse","url":"https://www.researchwithrutgers.org/en/publications/change-centric-management-of-versions-in-an-xml-warehouse/","authors":["Amélie Marian","Serge Abiteboul","Grégory Cobéna","Laurent Mignet"],"year":2001,"abstract":"A structured-document precursor for cross-version lineage. The work stores evolving XML snapshots through completed deltas and persistent identifiers, allowing changes between successive versions to be represented without treating each document snapshot as unrelated.","venue":"VLDB 2001"}
{"title":"Detecting Changes in XML Documents","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICDE.2002.994696","doi":"10.1109/ICDE.2002.994696","authors":["Grégory Cobéna","Serge Abiteboul","Amélie Marian"],"year":2002,"abstract":"Presents an XML differencing algorithm that matches nodes between old and new document versions and detects operations including subtree moves. Persistent identifiers and structural matching make it relevant to element correspondence across revisions, although the domain is structured XML rather than multimodal attachments.","venue":"ICDE 2002"}
{"title":"X-Diff: an effective change detection algorithm for XML documents","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICDE.2003.1260818","doi":"10.1109/ICDE.2003.1260818","year":2003,"abstract":"Detects structural changes between versions of XML documents under an unordered-tree model. It is relevant as a transfer mechanism for structural element correspondence and semantic differencing across document revisions.","venue":"ICDE 2003"}
{"title":"Change Detection In XML Documents","url":"https://www.nist.gov/publications/change-detection-xml-documents","authors":["K. H. Lee","Y. C. Choy","S. B. Cho","Xiao Tang","V. R. McCrary"],"year":2003,"abstract":"Uses top-down and bottom-up XML matching to identify insertions, deletions, updates, copies, and moves between document versions. The operation-level correspondence is useful precedent for revision lineage, while not addressing page regions or multimodal content directly.","venue":"International Workshop on XML-Based Data Management"}
{"title":"Schema-less, semantics-based change detection for XML documents","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-30480-7_29","doi":"10.1007/978-3-540-30480-7_29","authors":["Shuohao Zhang","Curtis Dyreson","Richard T. Snodgrass"],"year":2004,"abstract":"Associates XML elements across successive versions without requiring a schema and uses semantics to preserve identifiers through substantial structural change. It is particularly relevant to stable element identity when revisions alter document organization.","venue":"WISE 2004"}
{"title":"Temporal queries and version management in XML-based document archives","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.datak.2007.08.002","doi":"10.1016/j.datak.2007.08.002","authors":["Fusheng Wang","Carlo Zaniolo"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Studies storage and querying of multi-version XML document histories, including queries over document content and its evolution. It provides transfer evidence for version-aware element histories but does not cover spatial document regions or multimodal attachments.","venue":"Data & Knowledge Engineering"}
{"title":"Temporal and multi-versioned XML documents: A survey","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ipm.2013.08.003","doi":"10.1016/j.ipm.2013.08.003","authors":["Sidra Faisal","Mansoor Sarwar"],"year":2014,"abstract":"Surveys temporal and multi-version XML research, including change detection, version storage, and querying of evolving document elements. It is useful background for separating snapshot identity, change representation, and persistent element history.","venue":"Information Processing & Management"}
{"title":"Bridging the gap between tracking and detecting changes in XML","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/spe.2305","doi":"10.1002/spe.2305","authors":["Paolo Ciancarini","Angelo Di Iorio","Carlo Marchetti","Michele Schirinzi","Fabio Vitali"],"year":2016,"abstract":"Connects explicit change tracking with post-hoc differencing so that higher-level document changes can be represented rather than only low-level text edits. This is relevant to revision semantics and element lineage, though it operates on XML rather than rendered multimodal documents.","venue":"Software: Practice and Experience"}
{"title":"The Rationale of PROV","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.websem.2015.04.001","doi":"10.1016/j.websem.2015.04.001","authors":["Luc Moreau","Paul Groth","James Cheney","Timothy Lebo","Simon Miles"],"year":2015,"published":"2015-12-01","abstract":"Explains the design rationale of the W3C PROV model, in which entities, activities, agents, and derivation relations describe how artifacts came to be. This is transfer evidence for representing version and evidence lineage, but it does not prescribe page-, region-, cell-, or span-level document grounding.","venue":"Journal of Web Semantics"}
{"title":"The Open Provenance Model core specification (v1.1)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.future.2010.07.005","doi":"10.1016/j.future.2010.07.005","authors":["Luc Moreau","Ben Clifford","Juliana Freire","Joe Futrelle","Yolanda Gil","Paul Groth","Natalia Kwasnikowska","Simon Miles","Paolo Missier","Jim Myers","Beth Plale","Yogesh Simmhan","Eric Stephan","Jan Van den Bussche"],"year":2011,"abstract":"Defines a graph-based provenance representation intended to exchange causal histories across systems. Its entity and process relationships are a transferable substrate for hierarchical version lineage, but spatial document coordinates are outside its scope.","venue":"Future Generation Computer Systems"}
{"title":"Why and Where: A Characterization of Data Provenance","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-44503-X_20","doi":"10.1007/3-540-44503-X_20","authors":["Peter Buneman","Sanjeev Khanna","Wang-Chiew Tan"],"year":2001,"abstract":"Introduces distinct notions of why-provenance and where-provenance for database query results, formalizing both justification and source location. This is transfer evidence for exact source attribution, not direct evidence for multimodal documents or revised attachments.","venue":"ICDT 2001"}
{"title":"Tracing the lineage of view data in a warehousing environment","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/357775.357777","doi":"10.1145/357775.357777","authors":["Yingwei Cui","Jennifer Widom","Janet L. Wiener"],"year":2000,"published":"2000-06-01","abstract":"Defines lineage for derived warehouse data and develops methods for tracing an output item back to the source items that contributed to it. The fine-grained source-to-result relationship is transferable to evidence provenance but does not itself model document regions or versions.","venue":"ACM Transactions on Database Systems"}
{"title":"Provenance Semirings","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1265530.1265535","doi":"10.1145/1265530.1265535","authors":["Todd J. Green","Grigoris Karvounarakis","Val Tannen"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Provides an algebraic framework that annotates data with provenance and propagates those annotations through queries. It is foundational transfer work for compositional evidence lineage, but its identifiers refer to database data rather than document pages, cells, regions, or answer spans.","venue":"PODS 2007"}
{"title":"Demonstration of ProvSQL Update Provenance through Temporal Databases","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3736229.3736253","doi":"10.1145/3736229.3736253","authors":["Albert Ariel Widiaatmaja","Belkis Djeffal","Ashish Dandekar","Pierre Senellart"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-09-02","abstract":"Demonstrates provenance for database INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations together with temporal history and time-travel behavior. It is transfer evidence for provenance across revisions, rather than direct validation of document or workbook element lineage.","venue":"ProvenanceWeek 2025"}
{"title":"CiteVQA: Benchmarking Evidence Attribution for Trustworthy Document Intelligence","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12882","arxiv_id":"2605.12882","authors":["Dongsheng Ma","Jiayu Li","Zhengren Wang","Yijie Wang","Jiahao Kong","Weijun Zeng","Jutao Xiao","Jie Yang","Wentao Zhang","Bin Wang","Conghui He"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-05-13","abstract":"Benchmarks document question answering in which a system must provide an answer together with element-level bounding-box citations to the supporting source regions. It directly evaluates answer attribution inside document pages, but does not add cross-version attachment lineage.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"DocAttriBench: Benchmarking Answer Grounding in Document Visual Question Answering","url":"https://iris.unimore.it/handle/11380/1415609","authors":["Luca De Grandis","Silvia Cappelletti","William Raccagni","Marcella Cornia","Lorenzo Baraldi","Rita Cucchiara"],"year":2026,"abstract":"Benchmarks document VQA answer attribution at the level of document elements, including text blocks, tables, and images. It evaluates both answer correctness and whether the predicted answer is grounded in the correct supporting element.","venue":"British Machine Vision Conference (BMVC 2026)"}
{"title":"BBox DocVQA: A Large Scale Bounding Box Grounded Dataset for Enhancing Reasoning in Document Visual Question Answer","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15090","arxiv_id":"2511.15090","authors":["Wenhan Yu","Wang Chen","Guanqiang Qi","Weikang Li","Yang Li","Lei Sha","Deguo Xia","Jizhou Huang"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-11-19","abstract":"Extends document VQA with bounding-box grounding so that supporting regions are explicitly associated with answers. The dataset includes single- and multi-region as well as single- and multi-page evidence cases, making it directly relevant to region-level provenance.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"DocScope: Benchmarking Verifiable Reasoning for Trustworthy Long-Document Understanding","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.08888","arxiv_id":"2605.08888","authors":["Xiang Feng","Jiawei Zhou","Zhangfeng Huang","Kewei Wang","Shanshan Ye","Jinxin Hu","Zulong Chen","Yong Luo","Jing Zhang"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-05-09","abstract":"Represents verifiable long-document reasoning through a structured trajectory containing evidence pages, supporting evidence regions, factual statements, and a final answer. This directly exercises a page-to-region-to-claim-to-answer hierarchy, although attachment-version identity is not part of the representation.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"HierDoc: Hierarchical Page-to-Region Evidence Routing for Long-Document Visual Question Answering","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.29638","arxiv_id":"2607.29638","authors":["Rongjian Gu","Wengang Zhou","Junyu Xiong","Yonghui Wang","Bing Yin","Bei Wang","Houqiang Li"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-07-31","abstract":"Uses hierarchical evidence routing that first identifies relevant pages and then selects supporting semantic regions within those pages. Region representations can include crops, OCR text, and table content, making the work directly relevant to hierarchical document evidence localization.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"Towards Self-Explainable Document Visual Question Answering with Chain-of-Explanation Predictions","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06058","arxiv_id":"2605.06058","authors":["Kjetil Indrehus","Adrian Duric","Changkyu Choi","Ali Ramezani-Kebrya"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-05-07","abstract":"Introduces document VQA reasoning that explicitly identifies relevant evidence and localizes an answer region before producing the answer. The approach is pertinent to making document answers auditable at source-region level.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"Spatially Grounded Explanations in Vision Language Models for Document Visual Question Answering","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.12490","arxiv_id":"2507.12490","doi":"10.1007/978-3-032-04354-2_3","authors":["Maximiliano Hormazábal Lagos","Héctor Cerezo-Costas","Dimosthenis Karatzas"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-07-15","abstract":"Grounds explanatory rationales for document VQA in spatial subregions of the source document and constrains answering to selected evidence areas. It directly connects language reasoning with localized page evidence.","venue":"CLEF 2025"}
{"title":"ARIAL: An Agentic Framework for Document VQA with Precise Answer Localization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18192","arxiv_id":"2511.18192","authors":["Ahmad Mohammadshirazi","Pinaki Prasad Guha Neogi","Dheeraj Kulshrestha","Rajiv Ramnath"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-11-22","abstract":"Combines document question answering with explicit spatial answer localization through text-to-region alignment. It evaluates both textual answers and localization, directly addressing the need to preserve precise source coordinates alongside an answer.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"BoundingDocs: a Unified Dataset for Document Question Answering with Spatial Annotations","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.03403","arxiv_id":"2501.03403","doi":"10.1007/s10032-025-00563-5","authors":["Simone Giovannini","Fabio Coppini","Andrea Gemelli","Simone Marinai"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-01-06","abstract":"Associates document question-answer pairs with bounding boxes indicating the exact position of relevant source content. The spatial annotations make it useful for evaluating region-level provenance, while version lineage and workbook cells remain outside its scope.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"MAGE-RAG: Multigranular Adaptive Graph Evidence for Agentic Multimodal RAG in Long-Document QA","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.15906","arxiv_id":"2606.15906","authors":["Yilong Zuo","Xunkai Li","Jing Yuan","Qiangqiang Dai","Hongchao Qin","Ronghua Li"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-06-14","abstract":"Models long documents as an evidence graph containing page- and element-level nodes with relationships such as containment, reading order, layout adjacency, and section hierarchy. Unlike page-only retrieval, it exposes finer-grained document elements for evidence selection, though it does not address cross-version lineage.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"From Rows to Reasoning: A Retrieval-Augmented Multimodal Framework for Spreadsheet Understanding","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08741","arxiv_id":"2601.08741","authors":["Anmol Gulati","Sahil Sen","Waqar Sarguroh","Kevin Paul"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-01-13","abstract":"Targets enterprise spreadsheets with multiple linked sheets and multimodal content such as embedded images. It builds retrieval units at multiple spreadsheet granularities, providing direct evidence for workbook-level multimodal understanding but not comprehensive revision provenance.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"SpreadsheetLLM: Encoding Spreadsheets for Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.09025","arxiv_id":"2407.09025","authors":["Yuzhang Tian","Jianbo Zhao","Haoyu Dong","Junyu Xiong","Shiyu Xia","Mengyu Zhou","Yun Lin","José Cambronero","Yeye He","Shi Han","Dongmei Zhang"],"year":2024,"published":"2024-07-12","abstract":"Develops spreadsheet-specific encodings that preserve cell addresses, values, formats, and structural relationships while compressing large sheets for language models. It advances spreadsheet structure understanding but does not establish cell lineage across workbook revisions.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"SpreadsheetBench: Towards Challenging Real World Spreadsheet Manipulation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.14991","arxiv_id":"2406.14991","doi":"10.52202/079017-3007","authors":["Zeyao Ma","Bohan Zhang","Jing Zhang","Jifan Yu","Xiaokang Zhang","Xiaohan Zhang","Sijia Luo","Xi Wang","Jie Tang"],"year":2024,"published":"2024-06-21","abstract":"Benchmarks manipulation of real-world spreadsheets containing multiple sheets, multiple tables, non-standard relationships, and non-textual elements. It is useful for assessing workbook complexity beyond isolated clean tables, although provenance across versions is not a benchmark target.","venue":"NeurIPS 2024 Datasets and Benchmarks Track"}
{"title":"Back to the Future: A workbook time machine for spread sheet creation benchmarks","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07873","arxiv_id":"2608.07873","authors":["Mansi Uniyal","Agamdeep Singh","Ananya Singha","Priyanshu Gupta","Mukul Singh","Gust Verbruggen","Vu Le","Sumit Gulwani"],"year":2026,"published":"2026-08-08","abstract":"Constructs spreadsheet creation tasks by rewinding completed workbooks into earlier states, yielding trajectories that can include formulas, charts, pivot tables, and conditional formatting. The explicit before-and-after workbook states are relevant to revision research, though the work does not itself define persistent semantic cell lineage.","venue":"COLM 2026"}
{"title":"SheetCopilot: Bringing Software Productivity to the Next Level through Large Language Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19308","arxiv_id":"2305.19308","authors":["Hongxin Li","Jingran Su","Yuntao Chen","Qing Li","Zhaoxiang Zhang"],"year":2023,"published":"2023-05-30","abstract":"Uses language-model planning and spreadsheet operations to execute natural-language tasks over spreadsheets. Its benchmark and stateful manipulation framework provide evidence about spreadsheet interaction and structure, but not stable provenance across workbook revisions.","venue":"NeurIPS 2023"}
{"title":"InstructExcel: A Benchmark for Natural Language Instruction in Excel","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.14495","arxiv_id":"2310.14495","doi":"10.18653/v1/2023.findings-emnlp.265","authors":["Justin Payan","Swaroop Mishra","Mukul Singh","Carina Negreanu","Christian Poelitz","Chitta Baral","Subhro Roy","Rasika Chakravarthy","Benjamin Van Durme","Elnaz Nouri"],"year":2023,"published":"2023-10-23","abstract":"Benchmarks natural-language-driven Excel editing over a broad set of spreadsheet operations and real spreadsheet files. It provides evidence for workbook manipulation capabilities, but it is not a provenance or cross-version cell-lineage benchmark.","venue":"Findings of EMNLP 2023"}
{"title":"Large Language Models for Spreadsheets: Benchmarking Progress and Evaluating Performance with FLARE","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.17330","arxiv_id":"2506.17330","authors":["Simon Thorne"],"year":2025,"published":"2025-06-19","abstract":"Evaluates language models on spreadsheet reasoning tasks that include formula logic and auditing-oriented capabilities. It is useful for characterizing workbook reasoning gaps, but does not validate comprehensive versioned cell provenance.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"Robust Table Detection and Structure Recognition from Heterogeneous Document Images","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.09056","arxiv_id":"2203.09056","doi":"10.1016/j.patcog.2022.109006","authors":["Chixiang Ma","Weihong Lin","Lei Sun","Qiang Huo"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Performs table detection and structural reconstruction on heterogeneous document images, including difficult structures involving spanning cells and blank areas. The explicit recovery of cell topology is relevant to preserving source-cell semantics before downstream grounding.","venue":"Pattern Recognition"}
{"title":"Split, Embed and Merge: An accurate table structure recognizer","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.05214","arxiv_id":"2107.05214","doi":"10.1016/j.patcog.2022.108565","authors":["Zhenrong Zhang","Jianshu Zhang","Jun Du","Fengren Wang"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Recognizes complex table structures by first predicting fine-grained grids and then merging them into logical cells, using both visual and textual information. This is directly relevant to recovering spanning and merged-cell structure needed for precise cell grounding.","venue":"Pattern Recognition"}
{"title":"Table Structure Recognition and Form Parsing by End-to-End Object Detection and Relation Parsing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patcog.2022.108946","doi":"10.1016/j.patcog.2022.108946","authors":["Xiao-Hui Li","Fei Yin","He-Sen Dai","Cheng-Lin Liu"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Jointly detects table or form components and predicts structural relations among cells and other elements. The relation-based representation is relevant to retaining logical table structure rather than reducing a document to unstructured OCR text.","venue":"Pattern Recognition"}
{"title":"A large-scale dataset for end-to-end table recognition in the wild","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-023-01985-8","year":2023,"published":"2023-02-23","abstract":"Provides document-table annotations that include table-body location, cell spatial position, logical row and column coordinates, text, and row or column spanning information. These annotations directly support evaluation of structural preservation needed for later cell-level provenance.","venue":"Scientific Data"}
{"title":"A Prototype Document Image Analysis System for Technical Journals","url":"https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/csearticles/33/","doi":"10.1109/2.144436","authors":["George Nagy","Sharad C. Seth","Mahesh Viswanathan"],"year":1992,"abstract":"An early document-image analysis system that combines page segmentation and structural parsing for technical journal pages. It demonstrates that preservation of page organization and document structure was an explicit document-analysis problem well before modern multimodal models.","venue":"IEEE Computer"}
{"title":"High Performance Document Layout Analysis","url":"https://www.dfki.de/en/web/research/projects-and-publications/publication/2000","authors":["Thomas M. Breuel"],"year":2003,"abstract":"Develops robust geometric and statistical methods for document page layout analysis. It is foundational evidence for segmenting heterogeneous pages into meaningful regions before reading-order reconstruction or region-level evidence grounding.","venue":"Symposium on Document Image Understanding Technology"}
{"title":"A machine-learning approach for analyzing document layout structures with two reading orders","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patcog.2008.03.014","doi":"10.1016/j.patcog.2008.03.014","authors":["Chung-Chih Wu","Chien-Hsing Chou","Fu Chang"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Analyzes document layout while explicitly modeling pages that use different reading-order conventions, including horizontal and vertical text organization. It is relevant to preserving relationships among text lines and regions in complex scanned documents.","venue":"Pattern Recognition"}
{"title":"Abstract argumentation for reading order detection","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2644866.2644883","doi":"10.1145/2644866.2644883","authors":["Stefano Ferilli","Domenico Grieco","Domenico Redavid","Floriana Esposito"],"year":2014,"abstract":"Models reading-order decisions among document-page components using an abstract-argumentation formulation. The work directly concerns reconstruction of semantic ordering relationships between detected regions rather than region detection alone.","venue":"DocEng 2014"}
{"title":"XYLayoutLM: Towards Layout-Aware Multimodal Networks For Visually-Rich Document Understanding","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.06947","arxiv_id":"2203.06947","authors":["Zhangxuan Gu","Changhua Meng","Ke Wang","Jun Lan","Weiqiang Wang","Ming Gu","Liqing Zhang"],"year":2022,"published":"2022-03-14","abstract":"A layout-aware multimodal document model that explicitly addresses reading order and two-dimensional page structure in visually rich documents. Its treatment of ordered layout elements is relevant to preserving semantic document structure for later grounding.","venue":"CVPR 2022"}
{"title":"Reading order detection in visually-rich documents with multi-modal layout-aware relation prediction","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patcog.2024.110314","doi":"10.1016/j.patcog.2024.110314","authors":["Liang Qiao","Can Li","Zhanzhan Cheng","Yunlu Xu","Yi Niu","Xi Li"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Predicts reading-order relations using visual, semantic, and positional information from document elements. It directly evaluates ordering relationships in complex visually rich layouts, supporting structural preservation beyond independent region detection.","venue":"Pattern Recognition"}
{"title":"Rethinking Reading Order: Toward Generalizable Document Understanding with LLM-based Relation Modeling","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.eacl-long.192/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2026.eacl-long.192","authors":["Weishi Wang","Hengchang Hu","Daniel Dahlmeier"],"year":2026,"abstract":"Uses language-model-based relation modeling to infer global reading order and inter-element layout relationships. The work is relevant to hierarchical document structure because it models dependencies between elements rather than treating OCR blocks independently.","venue":"EACL 2026"}
{"title":"Resolving References in Visually-Grounded Dialogue via Text Generation","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.13430","arxiv_id":"2309.13430","doi":"10.18653/v1/2023.sigdial-1.43","authors":["Bram Willemsen","Livia Qian","Gabriel Skantze"],"year":2023,"published":"2023-09-23","abstract":"Transfer-domain work on resolving conversational references to visual entities. It generates descriptions of intended referents and uses vision-language grounding to resolve them, offering a mechanism potentially portable to message-to-attachment references but no workplace-document validation.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2023"}
{"title":"The PhotoBook Dataset: Building Common Ground through Visually-Grounded Dialogue","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P19-1184/","doi":"10.18653/v1/P19-1184","authors":["Janosch Haber","Tim Baumgärtner","Ece Takmaz","Lieke Gelderloos","Elia Bruni","Raquel Fernández"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Introduces visually grounded dialogues in which participants repeatedly refer to images while building common ground, enabling study of reference chains over conversational context. This is transfer evidence for cross-modal reference resolution rather than document-attachment grounding.","venue":"ACL 2019"}
{"title":"Refer, Reuse, Reduce: Generating Subsequent References in Visual and Conversational Contexts","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.353/","authors":["Ece Takmaz","Mario Giulianelli","Sandro Pezzelle","Arabella Sinclair","Raquel Fernández"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Studies how referring expressions evolve as visual entities are mentioned repeatedly in dialogue and models generation using both conversational and visual context. The resulting reference mechanisms are transferable to attachment references but are evaluated on image dialogue rather than workplace documents.","venue":"EMNLP 2020"}
{"title":"Phrase Grounding by Soft-Label Chain Conditional Random Field","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.00301","arxiv_id":"1909.00301","doi":"10.18653/v1/D19-1515","authors":["Jiacheng Liu","Julia Hockenmaier"],"year":2019,"published":"2019-09-01","abstract":"Transfer-domain phrase grounding that maps textual entity mentions to image regions while modeling dependencies among multiple mentions. It supplies a region-localization mechanism but does not establish document-specific or message-to-attachment grounding.","venue":"EMNLP-IJCNLP 2019"}
{"title":"Bridging the Gap between Expression and Scene Text for Referring Expression Comprehension (Student Abstract)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v36i11.21597","doi":"10.1609/aaai.v36i11.21597","authors":["Yuqi Bu","Jiayuan Xie","Liuwu Li","Qiong Liu","Yi Cai"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Transfer-domain referring-expression work that incorporates scene text and associates textual content with corresponding visual regions before grounding a target expression. It is adjacent to screenshots and text-heavy attachments, but does not evaluate document rows, cells, or workplace messages.","venue":"AAAI 2022"}
{"title":"Towards Unsupervised Referring Expression Comprehension with Visual Semantic Parsing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.knosys.2023.111318","doi":"10.1016/j.knosys.2023.111318","authors":["Yaodong Wang","Zhong Ji","Di Wang","Yanwei Pang","Xuelong Li"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Transfer-domain work on grounding referring expressions in natural images using visual-semantic structure without fully supervised target-region labels. Its semantic parsing ideas may carry over to attachment grounding, but the evaluation domain is not documents.","venue":"Knowledge-Based Systems"}
{"title":"RefDetector: A Simple Yet Effective Matching-based Method for Referring Expression Comprehension","url":"https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i8.32866","doi":"10.1609/aaai.v39i8.32866","authors":["Yabing Wang","Zhuotao Tian","Zheng Qin","Sanping Zhou","Le Wang"],"year":2025,"abstract":"Transfer-domain referring-expression comprehension using matching between language and candidate visual regions. It is relevant as a generic grounding mechanism but provides no validation for email language, document regions, tables, or cells.","venue":"AAAI 2025"}
{"title":"KnowDR-REC: Auditing Knowledge-Conditioned Visual Grounding in Referring Expression Comprehension","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-acl.1923/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2026.findings-acl.1923","authors":["Guanghao Jin","Jingpei Wu","Tianpei Guo","Yiyi Niu","Weidong Zhou","Linyi Yang","Guoyang Liu"],"year":2026,"abstract":"Audits referring-expression grounding under knowledge-conditioned settings, including entity resolution, spatial grounding, and rejection of unsupported or hallucinated matches. This is transfer evidence for robust reference resolution, not direct workplace attachment evaluation.","venue":"Findings of ACL 2026"}
{"title":"Entity-Enhanced Adaptive Reconstruction Network for Weakly Supervised Referring Expression Grounding","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/TPAMI.2022.3186410","doi":"10.1109/TPAMI.2022.3186410","authors":["Xuejing Liu","Liang Li","Shuhui Wang","Zheng-Jun Zha","Zechao Li","Qi Tian","Qingming Huang"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Transfer-domain work on weakly supervised grounding of language expressions to visual entities and regions. It contributes visual-language alignment machinery but does not establish grounding into document-specific structural elements or revised attachments.","venue":"IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence"}
{"title":"Challenges in Information-Seeking QA: Unanswerable Questions and Paragraph Retrieval","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11915","arxiv_id":"2010.11915","doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.acl-long.118","authors":["Akari Asai","Eunsol Choi"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Transfer-domain question-answering work that analyzes unanswerable questions together with evidence retrieval. It is relevant to distinguishing genuine evidence absence from successful evidence discovery, although it does not address multimodal document parsing failures or spatial provenance.","venue":"ACL 2021"}
{"title":"I Could've Asked That: Reformulating Unanswerable Questions","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.242/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.242","authors":["Wenting Zhao","Ge Gao","Claire Cardie","Alexander M. Rush"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Studies document-grounded question answering when the original question is unanswerable and explores reformulating it into answerable alternatives. It offers transfer evidence for explicit handling of evidence absence, while parser failure and spatial grounding are outside its main scope.","venue":"EMNLP 2024"}
